// TEST: Struct Where Array Form
// ============================================================================
// R2 of the T4 where-ruling, the other accepted spelling: the owner's "an
// array of predicates". `where [p1, p2]` arrives as ONE conjunct whose top
// node is a literal ExprArrayLit and flattens in Ast.structConjuncts exactly
// as the tuple form does (structs/043).
//
// R3 — LITERAL ONLY. There is deliberately NO all-true folding of an
// array-VALUED expression: only a literal `[...]` written at the top of a
// conjunct flattens. A non-literal array-valued conjunct stays the
// StructWhereNotBool error of structs/031. The reason is drift: all-true
// folding would make `where p` and `where (p,)` equivalent for arbitrary
// expressions, which is precisely what the single shared conjunct definition
// (Ast.fs:685, "so the two worlds cannot drift") exists to prevent.
//
// Same struct and same solution as structs/016 and structs/043: three
// spellings, one enumeration.
// ============================================================================
struct Ranged {
    lo: Int,
    hi: Int,
    mid: Int
} where [lo <= mid, mid <= hi]
let r = Ranged { lo = 1, mid = 5, hi = 9 }
let m = r.mid
// EXPECT: m = 5
